                  <provenance><orgName role="fmo" key="mlgbOrg_100">Cambridge, Cambridgeshire.
                        Gonville Hall</orgName><p>‘On the old Boards (out of sight) was found the
                        following inscription in cipher: ‘Gnotum sit omnibus quod ego Iohanne[s]
                        [C]anonicus Ci-trtonie dedi hunc librum Alano de camera alumpno meo a[n]no
                        Domini M. CC. L. viii die ueneris ante natiuitatem beate uirginis Marie ita
                        quidem quod illum m[...] perpetuo precario possideam. Val.’’ (Thomson, notes
                        towards a new catalogue, citing notes of M. R. James)</p><p>Cautiones on
                        fol. 248r, fol. 248v, and rear pastedown, including (on pastedown):
                        ‘Supplementum M. Henrici Thomston cuius principale est ?uolumen paruum.’
                        Henry de Thompston bequeathed his law books to Gonville Hall, of which this
                        was perhaps one</p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Perhaps Henry de Thompston (d. 1416), fellow
                     of Gonville Hall</provenance>
